      "2": {
        "attributes": {
          "font-size": {
            "type": "number",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "INDICES\n\nThe Indices node outputs the indices of an collection. This gives you a nice aray to adress each indice or the incomming collection.\nThis sounds incredibly complicated, we know, but the example might shine some light on why you might need this node.\n\nIn the example we create an overcomplicated array by using 5 different oscillators. We flip them with  the 1-x node and insert them one after another using Size and Insert. Now that we have our mess we want to write these values to the radii of the circles. To do this we create a float array and write our complex array to this. To write to the array we need to know where to write to. This is where the Indice comes into play. We have 10 values in our array so Indices outputs an array of 0 to 9 (since 0 is the first index). We use the indices for the index and we're done."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 189.4035186767578,
          "width": 1046.9403076171875,
          "x": -86.28985595703125,
          "y": 26.140811920166016
